===== code optimization round 2 =====
Time is tight (10 min). Let me look at ops.
Worse; reverting that and trying a row-blocked register version.
Also worse. Restoring the original loop.
Original restored (~25us). Next: remove the per-iteration 50-double copy with a sliding window buffer.
Verified, but no measurable gain. Last try: explicit NEON with all 25 accumulator registers live.
Same as the compiler's code, so the loop looks latency-bound (a 50-deep FMA chain). Trying split chains that still fit in registers:
Worse. Nothing beat the baseline, so I'm putting both files back to the round-1 sources.
None of this round's experiments beat the round-1 solver, so I left the sources as they were. After restoring them, `make verify` prints `VERIFY OK` and `make bench` gives about 25.2 us per solve. The round-1 figure of 27.3 us was a noisier run of the same code. I tried five changes and kept none:

- **Clang `unroll(full)` on the dense 50x50 loop:** no change.
- **Splitting each row's sum into two halves by column:** slower, 36 us; the doubled accumulators spill out of registers.
- **Blocks of 10 rows kept in registers:** slower, 35 us.
- **Sliding-window buffer to drop the 50-double copy each iteration:** it verified, but saved nothing measurable (24.9 vs 25.0 us).
- **Hand-written NEON code with all 50 sums in 25 registers:** the same speed as the plain loop, so the compiler already produces this.
- **NEON with two sums per row split across two row halves:** slower, 28 us.

My best guess at the limit, which I didn't confirm by reading the generated assembly: each iteration's matrix product is a chain of 50 dependent multiply-adds per row, and every iteration needs the previous one's result. Breaking that chain needs more registers than the chip has. Since this round gained essentially nothing, the pipeline should stop here.

FOMTOOLS-SUMMARY-BEGIN
Initial (this round) vs final whole-solve mean_us: 27.318 us -> 25.17 us. The same code was re-measured, so this is benchmark noise, not a real gain; there are no source changes this round. Against the translated solver: 329.159 us -> 25.17 us, about 13.1x overall.

Final profile (`make profile`, hottest first; the profiled build runs at 45.5 us because of instrumentation overhead):
- step_t0 (soft-threshold plus call overhead, includes the nested t0_matvec): 166429 us, 53.26%
- t0_matvec (dense 50x50 matrix-vector product, nested in step_t0): 126823 us, 40.58%
- state_update (copy of the 50-double upper half): 19260 us, 6.16%

Optimizations in place (all from round 1, kept unchanged):
- Collapsed products. t0_state = [0, W] and t0_state__d_gamma = [-W, W], so the t0 rhs is W @ (x1 + gamma*(x1 - x0)): one 50x50 product instead of a sparse 50x100 product plus a dense 50x100 product.
- Dense column-major W with coefficients inlined as a static const table. W is mostly dense, so the loop over rows vectorizes.
- Hoisted parameter term. t0_p_p0 @ p0 is constant across iterations and is computed once per solve as the bias.
- Selection rows done as copies. The su block is a pure shift, so x0 <- x1 is one memcpy and t0 is written straight into the upper half of the next state (ping-pong buffers, no zeroing passes).
- Soft-threshold fused into the step routine, with the residual computed only on the last iteration. There is no tolerance (tol=None, max_iter only), so the residual is needed only for the final output.

Tried this round and reverted (none faster):
- Full unroll of the loop over rows: no change.
- Two independent column chains per row: slower, 36 us (register spills).
- 10-row register blocks: slower, 35 us.
- Sliding-window buffer to drop the state_update copy: verified, but no measurable gain.
- Explicit NEON with 25 accumulator registers: same speed, since the compiler already generates it.
- NEON with two chains per row and two row halves: slower, 28 us.

Likely limit, not confirmed from the assembly: a dependency chain of 50 multiply-adds per row inside a strictly sequential iteration.

Blocked by rules:
- Running several solves at once (SIMD across parameter instances) to hide the FMA latency | the fixed one-solve-per-call API and headers | unknown, maybe 2x
- Single-precision W | dtype is fixed at float64 | unknown
FOMTOOLS-SUMMARY-END
